1
00:00:00,078 --> 00:00:07,120
In the next 17 minutes, I will give you an overview of the most important machine learning algorithms to help you decide which one is right for your problem.

2
00:00:07,374 --> 00:00:15,200
My name is Tim, and I have been a data scientist for over 10 years and taught all of these algorithms to hundreds of students in real-life machine learning boot camps.

3
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:18,240
There is a simple strategy for picking the right algorithm for your problem.

4
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:24,800
In 17 minutes, you will know how to pick the right one for any problem and get a basic intuition of each algorithm and how they relate to each other.

5
00:00:25,006 --> 00:00:31,588
My goal is to give as many of you as possible an intuitive understanding of the major machine learning algorithms to make you stop feeling overwhelmed.

6
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:43,840
According to Wikipedia, machine learning is a field of study in artificial intelligence concerned with the development and study of statistical algorithms that can learn from data and generalize to unseen data and thus perform tasks without explicit instructions.

7
00:00:44,110 --> 00:00:50,532
Much of the recent advancements in AI are driven by neural networks, which I hope to give you an intuitive understanding of by the end of this video.

8
00:00:50,990 --> 00:00:53,348
Let's divide machine learning into its subfields.

9
00:00:53,838 --> 00:00:58,720
Generally, machine learning is divided into two areas, supervised learning and unsupervised learning.

10
00:00:58,990 --> 00:01:09,924
Supervised learning is when we have a dataset with any number of independent variables, also called features or input variables, and a dependent variable, also called target or output variable, that is supposed to be predicted.

11
00:01:10,446 --> 00:01:20,100
We have a so-called training data set where we know the true values for the output variable, also called labels, that we can train our algorithm on to later predict the output variable for new unknown data.

12
00:01:20,366 --> 00:01:31,364
Examples could be predicting the price of a house, the output variable based on features of the house, say square footage, location, year of construction, etc . categorizing an object as a cat or a dog.

13
00:01:31,854 --> 00:01:37,760
the output variable or label based on features of the object, say height, weight, size of the ears, color of the eyes, etc.

14
00:01:38,094 --> 00:01:43,440
Unsupervised learning is basically any learning problem that is not supervised, so where no truth about the data is known.

15
00:01:43,662 --> 00:01:52,480
So where a supervised algorithm would be like showing a little kid what a typical cat looks like and what a typical dog looks like, and then giving it a new picture and asking it what animal it sees.

16
00:01:52,654 --> 00:02:01,360
An unsupervised algorithm would be giving a kid with no idea of what cats and dogs are, a pile of pictures of animals and asking it to group by similarity without any further instructions.

17
00:02:01,646 --> 00:02:09,124
Examples of unsupervised problems might be to sort all of your emails into three unspecified categories, which you can then later inspect and name as you wish.

18
00:02:09,646 --> 00:02:14,240
The algorithm will decide on its own how it will create those categories, also called clusters.

19
00:02:14,510 --> 00:02:18,884
Let's start with supervised learning, arguably the bigger and more important branch of machine learning.

20
00:02:19,214 --> 00:02:20,800
There are broadly two subcategories.

21
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:25,600
In regression, we want to predict a continuous numeric target variable for a given input variable.

22
00:02:25,838 --> 00:02:33,760
Using the example from before, it could be predicting the price of a house given any number of features of a house, and determining their relationship to the final price of the house.

23
00:02:34,030 --> 00:02:42,308
We might, for example, find out that square footage is directly proportional to the price, linear dependence, but that the age of the house has no influence on the price of the house.

24
00:02:42,574 --> 00:02:47,760
In classification, we try to assign a discrete categorical label, also called a class, to a data point.

25
00:02:47,950 --> 00:02:53,060
For example, we may want to assign the label spam or no spam to an email based on its content, sender, and so on.

26
00:02:53,518 --> 00:02:59,280
But we could also have more than two classes, for example, junk, primary social promotions, and updates, as Gmail does by default.

27
00:02:59,502 --> 00:03:05,380
Now let's dive into the actual algorithms, starting with the mother of all machine learning algorithms, linear regression.

28
00:03:05,838 --> 00:03:10,080
In general, supervised learning algorithms try to determine the relationship between two variables.

29
00:03:10,286 --> 00:03:12,560
We try to find the function that maps one to the other.

30
00:03:12,910 --> 00:03:20,160
Linear regression, in its simplest form, is trying to determine a linear relationship between two variables, namely the input and the output.

31
00:03:20,494 --> 00:03:26,884
We want to fit a linear equation to the data by minimizing the sum of squares of the distances between data points and the regression line.

32
00:03:27,246 --> 00:03:35,520
This simply minimizes the average distance of the real data to our predictive model, in this case the regression line, and should therefore minimize prediction errors for new data points.

33
00:03:35,694 --> 00:03:45,444
A simple example of a linear relationship might be the height and shoe size of a person, where the regression fit might tell us that for every one unit of shoe size increase, the person will be on average two inches taller.

34
00:03:45,774 --> 00:03:49,520
You can make your model more complex and fit multidimensional data to an output variable.

35
00:03:49,742 --> 00:03:55,600
In the example of the shoe size, you might, for example, want to include the gender, age, and ethnicity of the person to get an even better model.

36
00:03:55,760 --> 00:04:03,920
Many of the very fancy machine learning algorithms, including neural networks, are just extensions of this very simple idea, as I will show you later in the video.

37
00:04:04,238 --> 00:04:09,092
Logistic regression is a variant of linear regression and probably the most basic classification algorithm.

38
00:04:09,262 --> 00:04:19,360
Instead of fitting a line to two numerical variables with a presumably linear relationship, you now try to predict a categorical output variable using categorical or numerical input variables.

39
00:04:19,598 --> 00:04:20,800
Let's look at an example.

40
00:04:21,006 --> 00:04:26,240
We now want to predict one of two classes, for example, the gender of a person based on height and weight.

41
00:04:26,606 --> 00:04:28,868
So a linear regression wouldn't make much sense anymore.

42
00:04:29,198 --> 00:04:34,436
Instead of fitting a line to the data, we now fit a so called sigmoid function to the data, which looks like this.

43
00:04:34,958 --> 00:04:44,452
The equation will now not tell us about a linear relationship between two variables, but will now conveniently tell us the probability of a data point falling into a certain class given the value of the input variable.

44
00:04:44,814 --> 00:04:50,560
So for example, the likelihood of an adult person with a height of 180 centimeters being a man would be 80%.

45
00:04:51,470 --> 00:04:52,912
This is completely made up, of course.

46
00:04:53,358 --> 00:05:00,192
The K nearest neighbors algorithm, or KNN, is a very simple and intuitive algorithm that can be used for both regression and classification.

47
00:05:00,366 --> 00:05:02,592
It is a so-called non-parametric algorithm.

48
00:05:02,830 --> 00:05:06,884
The name means that we don't try to fit any equations and thus find any parameters of a model.

49
00:05:07,118 --> 00:05:08,752
So no true model fitting is necessary.

50
00:05:09,006 --> 00:05:14,912
The idea of KNN is simply that for any given new data point, we will predict the target to be the average of its K nearest neighbors.

51
00:05:15,086 --> 00:05:22,512
While this might seem very simple, this is actually a very powerful predictive algorithm, especially when relationships are more complicated than a simple linear relationship.

52
00:05:22,734 --> 00:05:30,432
In a classification example, we might say that the gender of a person will be the same as the majority of the five people closest in weight and height to the person in question.

53
00:05:30,766 --> 00:05:37,472
In a regression example, we might say that the weight of a person is the average weight of the three people closest in height and of chest circumference.

54
00:05:37,632 --> 00:05:39,140
This makes a ton of intuitive sense.

55
00:05:39,342 --> 00:05:42,112
You might realize that the number three seems a bit arbitrary, and it is.

56
00:05:42,382 --> 00:05:46,352
K is called a hyperparameter of the algorithm, and choosing the right k is an art.

57
00:05:46,512 --> 00:05:55,844
Choosing a very small number of k, say one or two, will lead to your model predicting your training data set very well, but not generalizing well to unseen data, this is called overfitting.

58
00:05:56,238 --> 00:06:00,964
Choosing a very large number, say 1000, will lead to a worse fit overall, this is called underfitting.

59
00:06:01,390 --> 00:06:04,512
The best number is somewhere in between and depends a lot on the problem at hand.

60
00:06:04,686 --> 00:06:09,712
Methods for finding the right hyperparameters include cross-validation but are beyond the scope of this video.

61
00:06:10,062 --> 00:06:17,952
Support vector machine is a supervised machine learning algorithm originally designed for classification tasks, but it can also be used for regression tasks.

62
00:06:18,254 --> 00:06:25,072
The core concept of the algorithm is to draw a decision boundary between data points that separates data points of the training set as well as possible.

63
00:06:25,326 --> 00:06:31,392
As the name suggests, a new unseen data point will be classified according to where it falls with respect to the decision boundary.

64
00:06:31,552 --> 00:06:36,832
Let's take this arbitrary example of trying to classify animals by their weight and the length of their nose.

65
00:06:37,070 --> 00:06:41,472
In this simple case of trying to classify cats and elephants, the decision boundary is a straight line.

66
00:06:41,774 --> 00:06:48,752
The SVM algorithm tries to find the line that separates the classes with the largest margin possible, that is maximizing the space between the different classes.

67
00:06:48,974 --> 00:06:53,392
This makes the decision boundary generalize well and less sensitive to noise and outliers in the training data.

68
00:06:53,550 --> 00:06:57,156
The so-called support vectors are the data points that sit on the edge of the margin.

69
00:06:57,454 --> 00:07:02,820
Knowing the support vectors is enough to classify new data points, which often makes the algorithm very memory efficient.

70
00:07:03,214 --> 00:07:06,916
One of the benefits of SVM is that it is very powerful in high dimensions.

71
00:07:07,310 --> 00:07:10,432
That is, if the number of features is large compared to the size of the data.

72
00:07:10,638 --> 00:07:13,952
In those higher dimensional cases, the decision boundary is called a hyperplane.

73
00:07:14,112 --> 00:07:21,764
Another feature that makes SVMs extremely powerful is the use of so-called kernel functions, which allow for the identification of highly complex nonlinear decision boundaries.

74
00:07:21,952 --> 00:07:30,272
Kernel functions are an implicit way to turn your original features into new, more complex features using the so-called kernel trick, which is beyond the scope of this video.

75
00:07:30,542 --> 00:07:38,180
This allows for efficient creation of nonlinear decision boundaries by creating complex new features, such as weight divided by height squared, also called the BMI.

76
00:07:38,318 --> 00:07:39,952
This is called implicit feature engineering.

77
00:07:40,206 --> 00:07:44,672
Neural networks take the idea of implicit feature engineering to the next level, as I will explain later.

78
00:07:44,846 --> 00:07:50,032
Possible kernel functions for SVMs are the linear, the polynomial, the RBF, and the sigmoid kernel.

79
00:07:50,192 --> 00:07:55,792
Another fairly simple classifier is the naive Bayes classifier that gets its name from Bayes' theorem, which looks like this.

80
00:07:56,046 --> 00:08:01,472
I believe it's easiest to understand naive Bayes with an example use case that it is often used for, spam filters.

81
00:08:01,870 --> 00:08:11,364
We can train our algorithm with a number of spam and non-spam emails and count the occurrences of different words in each class, and thereby calculate the probability of certain words appearing in spam emails and non spam emails.

82
00:08:11,662 --> 00:08:16,420
We can then quickly classify a new email based on the words it contains by using Bayes' theorem.

83
00:08:16,782 --> 00:08:20,196
We simply multiply the different probabilities of all words in the email together.

84
00:08:20,622 --> 00:08:27,712
This algorithm makes the false assumption that the probabilities of the different words appearing are independent of each other, which is why we call this classifier naive.

85
00:08:28,270 --> 00:08:36,740
This makes it very computationally efficient while still being a good approximation for many use cases such as spam classification and other text-based classification tasks.

86
00:08:37,166 --> 00:08:41,060
Decision trees are the basis of a number of more complex supervised learning algorithms.

87
00:08:41,422 --> 00:08:44,304
In its simplest form, a decision tree looks somewhat like this.

88
00:08:44,686 --> 00:08:50,404
The decision tree is basically a series of yes-no questions that allow us to partition a dataset in several dimensions.

89
00:08:50,670 --> 00:08:51,264
Here is an example.

90
00:08:51,504 --> 00:08:55,300
Decision tree for classifying people into high and low-risk patients for heart attacks.

91
00:08:55,598 --> 00:09:10,724
The goal of the decision tree algorithm is to create so-called leaf nodes at the bottom of the tree that are as pure as possible, meaning instead of randomly splitting the data, we try to find splits that lead to the resulting groups, or leaves, to be as pure as possible, which is to say that as few data points as possible are misclassified.

92
00:09:11,022 --> 00:09:18,544
While this might seem like a very basic and simple algorithm, which it is, we can turn it into a very powerful algorithm by combining many decision trees together.

93
00:09:18,704 --> 00:09:23,264
Combining many simple models to a more powerful, complex model is called an ensemble algorithm.

94
00:09:23,438 --> 00:09:29,668
One form of ensembling is bagging, where we train multiple models on different subsets of the training data using a method called bootstrapping.

95
00:09:30,062 --> 00:09:38,784
A famous version of this idea is called a random forest, where many decision trees vote on the classification of your data by majority vote of the different trees in the random forest.

96
00:09:39,086 --> 00:09:43,524
Random forests are very powerful estimators that can be used both for classification and regression.

97
00:09:43,950 --> 00:09:52,704
The randomness comes from randomly excluding features for different trees in the forest, which prevents overfitting and makes it much more robust because it removes correlation between the trees.

98
00:09:52,974 --> 00:10:04,144
Another type of ensemble method is called boosting, where instead of running many decision trees in parallel like for random forests, we train models in sequence, where each model focuses on fixing the errors made by the previous model.

99
00:10:04,334 --> 00:10:07,744
We combine a series of weak models in sequence, thus becoming a strong model.

100
00:10:07,904 --> 00:10:15,984
Because each sequential model tries to fix the errors of the previous model, boosted trees often get to higher accuracies than random forests, but are also more prone to overfitting.

101
00:10:16,110 --> 00:10:19,424
Its sequential nature makes it slower to train than random forests.

102
00:10:19,694 --> 00:10:25,984
Famous examples of boosted trees are AdaBoost, Gradient Boosting, and XGBoost, the details of which are beyond the scope of this video.

103
00:10:26,190 --> 00:10:29,024
Now let's get to the reigning king of AI, neural networks.

104
00:10:29,294 --> 00:10:32,144
To understand neural networks, let's look at logistic regression again.

105
00:10:32,398 --> 00:10:35,344
Say we have a number of features and are trying to predict a target class.

106
00:10:35,566 --> 00:10:42,144
The features might be pixel intensities of a digital image, and the target might be classifying the image as one of the digits from 0 to 9.

107
00:10:42,224 --> 00:10:56,784
Now for this particular case, you might see why this might be difficult to do with logistic regression, because say the number one doesn't look the same when different people write it, and even if the same person writes it several times, it will look slightly different each time and it won't be the exact same pixels illuminated for every instance of the number one.

108
00:10:57,024 --> 00:10:59,424
All of the instances of the number one have commonalities.

109
00:10:59,504 --> 00:11:09,252
However, like they all have a dominating vertical line and usually no crossing lines as other digits might have, and usually there are no circular shapes in the number one as there would be in the number eight or nine.

110
00:11:09,646 --> 00:11:14,544
However, the computer doesn't initially know about these more complex features, but only the pixel intensities.

111
00:11:14,798 --> 00:11:20,464
We could manually engineer these features by measuring some of these things and explicitly adding them as new features.

112
00:11:20,718 --> 00:11:29,904
But artificial neural networks, similarly to using a kernel function with a support vector machine, are designed to implicitly and automatically design these features for us, without any guidance from humans.

113
00:11:30,126 --> 00:11:34,464
We do this by adding additional layers of unknown variables between the input and output variables.

114
00:11:34,638 --> 00:11:40,224
In its simplest form, this is called a single layer perceptron, which is basically just a multi-feature regression task.

115
00:11:40,462 --> 00:11:45,092
Now if we add a hidden layer, the hidden variables in the middle layer represent some hidden unknown features.

116
00:11:45,390 --> 00:11:53,904
And instead of predicting the target variable directly, we try to predict these hidden features with our input features, and then try to predict the target variables with our new hidden features.

117
00:11:54,158 --> 00:12:07,172
In our specific example, we might be able to say that every time several pixels are illuminated next to each other, they represent a horizontal line, which can be a new feature to try and predict the digit in question, even though we never explicitly defined a feature called horizontal line.

118
00:12:07,598 --> 00:12:12,228
This is a much simplified view of what is actually going on, but hopefully this gets the point across.

119
00:12:12,622 --> 00:12:14,864
We don't usually know what the hidden features represent.

120
00:12:15,118 --> 00:12:18,624
We just train the neural network to predict the final target as well as possible.

121
00:12:18,894 --> 00:12:22,784
The hidden features we can design this way are limited in the case of the single hidden layer.

122
00:12:22,894 --> 00:12:25,984
But what if we add a layer and have the hidden layer predict another hidden layer?

123
00:12:26,094 --> 00:12:27,744
What if we now had even more layers?

124
00:12:28,014 --> 00:12:37,024
This is called deep learning and can result in very complex hidden features, so that might represent all kinds of complex information in the pictures, like the fact that there is a face in the picture.

125
00:12:37,486 --> 00:12:40,068
However, we will usually not know what the hidden features mean.

126
00:12:40,302 --> 00:12:42,180
We just know that they result in good predictions.

127
00:12:42,798 --> 00:12:49,408
All we have talked about so far is supervised learning, where we wanted to predict a specific target variable using some input variables.

128
00:12:49,806 --> 00:12:54,916
However, sometimes we don't have anything specific to predict and just want to find some underlying structure in our data.

129
00:12:55,278 --> 00:12:57,060
That's where unsupervised learning comes in.

130
00:12:57,390 --> 00:12:59,780
A very common unsupervised problem is clustering.

131
00:13:00,046 --> 00:13:04,260
It's easy to confuse clustering with classification, but they are conceptually very different.

132
00:13:04,558 --> 00:13:11,968
Classification is when we know the classes we want to predict and have training data with true labels available, shown as colors here, like pictures of cats and dogs.

133
00:13:12,302 --> 00:13:20,288
Clustering is when we don't have any labels and want to find unknown clusters just by looking at the overall structure of the data and trying to find potential clusters in the data.

134
00:13:20,494 --> 00:13:23,556
For example, we might look at a two-dimensional data set that looks like this.

135
00:13:23,854 --> 00:13:26,532
Any human will probably easily see three clusters here.

136
00:13:26,990 --> 00:13:30,368
But it's not always as straightforward as your data might also look like this.

137
00:13:30,862 --> 00:13:33,924
We don't know how many clusters there are because the problem is unsupervised.

138
00:13:34,350 --> 00:13:37,476
The most famous clustering algorithm is called k-means clustering.

139
00:13:37,934 --> 00:13:42,724
Just like for KNN, K is a hyperparameter and stands for the number of clusters you are looking for.

140
00:13:43,246 --> 00:13:50,116
Finding the right number of clusters again is an art and has a lot to do with your specific problem, and some trial and error in domain knowledge might be required.

141
00:13:50,510 --> 00:13:52,100
This is beyond the scope of this video.

142
00:13:52,526 --> 00:13:53,764
K-Means is very simple.

143
00:13:54,158 --> 00:14:00,196
You start by randomly selecting centers for your K clusters and assigning all data points to the cluster center closest to them.

144
00:14:00,622 --> 00:14:02,528
The clusters here are shown in blue and green.

145
00:14:02,798 --> 00:14:06,500
You then recalculate the cluster centers based on the data points now assigned to them.

146
00:14:06,926 --> 00:14:09,540
You can see the centers moving closer to the actual clusters.

147
00:14:09,966 --> 00:14:14,788
You then assign the data points again to the new cluster centers, followed by recalculating the cluster centers.

148
00:14:15,566 --> 00:14:19,168
You repeat this process until the centers of the clusters have stabilized.

149
00:14:19,534 --> 00:14:33,380
While k-means is the most famous and most common clustering algorithm, other algorithms exist, including some where you don't need to specify the number of clusters, like hierarchical clustering and db scan, which can find clusters of arbitrary shape, but I won't discuss them here.

150
00:14:33,646 --> 00:14:37,008
The last type of algorithm I will leave you with is dimensionality reduction.

151
00:14:37,262 --> 00:14:43,488
The idea of dimensionality reduction is to reduce the number of features or dimensions of your data set, keeping as much information as possible.

152
00:14:43,854 --> 00:14:51,968
Usually this group of algorithms does this by finding correlations between existing features and removing potentially redundant dimensions without losing much information.

153
00:14:52,334 --> 00:14:58,928
For example, do you really need a picture in high resolution to recognize the airplane in the picture, or can you reduce the number of pixels in the image?

154
00:14:59,150 --> 00:15:12,580
As such, dimensionality reduction will give you information about the relationships within your existing features, and it can also be used as a pre-processing step in your supervised learning algorithm to reduce the number of features in your dataset and make the algorithm more efficient and robust.

155
00:15:12,974 --> 00:15:17,088
An example algorithm is principal component analysis, or PCA.

156
00:15:17,422 --> 00:15:23,172
Let's say we are trying to predict types of fish based on several features like length, height, color, and number of teeth.

157
00:15:23,438 --> 00:15:33,008
When looking at the correlations of the different features, we might find that height and length are strongly correlated, and including both won't help the algorithm much, and might in fact hurt it by introducing noise.

158
00:15:33,230 --> 00:15:36,288
We can simply include a shape feature that is a combination of the two.

159
00:15:36,430 --> 00:15:42,500
This is actually extremely common in large datasets and allows us to reduce the number of features dramatically and still get good results.

160
00:15:42,990 --> 00:15:47,076
PCA does this by finding the directions in which most variants in the dataset is retained.

161
00:15:47,374 --> 00:15:50,276
In this example, the direction of most variants is a diagonal.

162
00:15:50,446 --> 00:15:54,928
This is called the first principal component, or PC, and can become our new shape feature.

163
00:15:55,182 --> 00:16:03,248
The second principal component is orthogonal to the first and only explains a small fraction of the variants of the dataset and can thus be excluded from our dataset in this case.

164
00:16:03,502 --> 00:16:13,248
In large datasets, we can do this for all features and rank them by explain variants and exclude any principal components that don't contribute much to the variants and thus wouldn't help much in our ML model.

165
00:16:13,422 --> 00:16:15,968
This was all common machine learning algorithms explained.

166
00:16:16,110 --> 00:16:23,940
If you are overwhelmed and don't know which algorithm you need, here is a great cheat sheet by ScikitLearn that will help you decide which algorithm is right for which type of problem.

167
00:16:24,302 --> 00:16:27,972
If you want a roadmap on how to learn machine learning, check out my video on that.

